{
  "note": "Scanned pairing codes and what a parser must make of each. The format is the one thing the Pi and the phone must agree on exactly, and they never run in the same process. Most of what is pinned here is refusals: a pairing code is an image anyone can tape over a display. \u0027outcome\u0027 is parsed / notAPairingCode / malformed, and the distinction between the last two matters at the scanner \u2014 one is a shrug, the other is worth telling the user about.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "minimal",
      "what": "The least a code can carry. Note what it still warns about: no crypto profile stated, no trust anchor, no proximity proof \u2014 silence is not conformance.",
      "input": "https://open.charging.cloud/evsim/pair#v=1\u0026host=192.168.4.1\u0026port=15118",
      "outcome": "parsed",
      "error": null,
      "payload": {
        "version": 1,
        "host": "192.168.4.1",
        "port": 15118,
        "transport": "tls",
        "protocols": [],
        "crypto": null,
        "nonConformant": false,
        "nonConformanceReason": null,
        "rootFingerprint": null,
        "meter": null,
        "totp": null,
        "evseId": null,
        "tariffId": null,
        "currency": null,
        "uiLanguage": null,
        "wifiSsid": null,
        "wifiPsk": null,
        "extra": {}
      },
      "warnings": [
        {
          "kind": "weakenedCrypto",
          "blocking": false
        },
        {
          "kind": "noTrustAnchor",
          "blocking": false
        },
        {
          "kind": "noProximityProof",
          "blocking": false
        }
      ]
    },
